{"answer":"No. Taking the nonnegative integer n=40 gives n^2+n+41=1681=41^2=41·41, which is composite. Thus the universal statement is false. This conclusion is self-contained and requires no external source.","approaches":[{"description":"Test the explicit boundary value n=40 and factor the resulting polynomial value.","id":"counterexample-at-forty","outcome":"candidate","reason":"It yields a direct counterexample, sufficient to refute a universal statement."},{"description":"Attempt a proof that the polynomial is prime for every nonnegative integer.","id":"universal-primality-proof","outcome":"rejected","reason":"The counterexample at n=40 has composite value, so no such proof can be valid."}],"change_log":[],"claims":[{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Direct substitution and arithmetic in evaluate-forty.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":[],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"value-at-forty","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"For n=40, n^2+n+41=1681.","step_ids":["evaluate-forty"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Explicit factorization in factor-value.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["value-at-forty"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"composite-at-forty","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"The value 1681 is composite, since 1681=41·41 and 41>1.","step_ids":["factor-value"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"A universal assertion is false when one member of its stated domain is a counterexample; applied in refute-universal.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["composite-at-forty"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"universal-statement-false","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"The assertion that n^2+n+41 is prime for every nonnegative integer n is false.","step_ids":["refute-universal"],"tool_ids":[]}],"open_questions":[],"proof_steps":[{"citations":[],"depends_on":[],"id":"evaluate-forty","justification":"Substitution gives 40^2+40+4